\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The massive tower}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{Keep #{p,\lambda} and the projectors of the
[blocking step](fdip-01CJ). For #{\mu>0}, take
##{Q_k=\sum_{j<k}\lambda^jP_j^{(k)}+\mu\lambda^k\bbI.}
At depth zero this is #{\mu\bbI}. At positive depth the lower
eigenvalues are #{\lambda^j+\mu\lambda^k} and the top eigenvalue
is #{\mu\lambda^k}, all positive. The coefficient shift of
\ref{fdip-00C1} gives
##{\frac{\lambda^{j+1}+\mu\lambda^k}{\lambda}
=\lambda^j+\mu\lambda^{k-1}\quad(j<k-1),}
and sends the top coefficient to #{\mu\lambda^{k-1}}.
These precisions form a tower. Its profile is
##{g(0)=\mu,\qquad g(s)=\lambda^{-s}+\mu\quad(s\ge1).}
The mass term acts on every field direction, including the constants.}

\p{If #{\lambda\ne1}, the finest eigenvalue
#{1+\mu\lambda^k} changes with depth, so this tower is not fixed.
If #{\lambda=1}, its lower eigenvalues are all #{1+\mu} and its
top eigenvalue is #{\mu}; it is then a fixed tower.}
